03034}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2024,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6959,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kellen Moore"},{"season":2025,"team":"NYG","distance":0.7663,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Mike Kafka"},{"season":2025,"team":"PHI","distance":0.8383,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kevin Patullo"},{"season":2023,"team":"CHI","distance":0.8502,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"CAR","distance":0.8512,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"HOU","distance":0.5058,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2018,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5842,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2019,"team":"DAL","distance":0.5964,"shared":18,"play_caller":null},{"season":2024,"team":"GB","distance":0.598,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2022,"team":"CHI","distance":0.6459,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Colts went 8-9 in 2024 and were outscored 427 to 377. The defense did most of the work, finishing 13th in EPA allowed while the offense ranked 24th in EPA per play. What set them apart was the 2nd lowest under center rate and the 3rd highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","offense":"The Colts offense was well below average by EPA per play. They were one of the most run-heavy teams in the league, passing less often than game situations would predict. Schematically, they stood out for the 2nd lowest under center rate and the 2nd lowest under center rate in the league. Anthony Richardson ran the passing game, with Michael Pittman leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Colts defense was a little above average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 10th and 25th. Kwity Paye was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Laiatu Latu, who had 4 sacks while playing only 55% of the snaps, after not being a regular on this defense last season. Schematically, they had the 3rd highest Cover 2 rate in the league.","special":"The Colts were one of the most aggressive teams on fourth down, going for it on 59% of their short-yardage chances in normal situations. Their kickers made 84% of field goals, which was a little below average. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the defensive line, about 27% of the money on the books. The offense also brought back more of its roster than most, with 84% of its snaps going to players who were on the team the year before.","players":"Anthony Richardson played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 1,814 yards. Michael Pittman was the clear top target with 23% of the looks and 808 receiving yards. Jonathan Taylor handled most of the work in the backfield with 76% of the carries. On defense, Kwity Paye led the team with 8 sacks and Nick Cross picked off 3 passes.","coaching":"Shane Steichen called the offense, and the defensive play caller for this season is not in the coaching data. Their offense looked most like the 2024 Eagles under Kellen Moore and the 2025 Giants under Mike Kafka. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Texans and the 2018 Cowboys.","trends":"The offense went from 16th in EPA per play in 2023 to 24th in 2024, a big step back. The defense moved from 21st in EPA allowed per play in 2023 to 13th in 2024, a big step forward. The biggest change in approach was eight man box rate, which went from 5% to 6%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2024. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}